{ @@HeaderStart@@Deflection@@HeaderEnd@@  Deflection is a tactical motif whose objective is to force a piece away from the defence of another piece or a key square.   The black queen guards the h4 square, which is a role of vital importance as from this square the white queen can force checkmate. By making a deflection sacrifice, White can put his queen on this key square: }
1. Rc8 Qxc8
{ the black queen cannot continue to guard h4, as the squares e7, f6 and g5 are all under White's control }
2. Qh4+ Kg8 3. Qh7# *

[Event "?"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "10. Deflection"]
[Black "Deflection #2"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "1r1q2rk/7p/7Q/8/2b5/6RP/5PPK/4R3 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]

{ Deflection, like all tactical motifs, can be combined with other elements, as illustrated in the following position. With the spectacular }
1. Re8 { The plight of the black queen is miserable: if } 1... Qb6
{ 1...Qc7  will be met by  2.Qf6+  followed by mate;   after  1...Qd4  @@StartBracket@@the only move that parries all the mating threats, but leaves the b8-rook undefended@@EndBracket@@ there is  2.Rgxg8+  Bxg8  3.Rxb8  , and White has a decisive advantage: the double attack  3...Qe5+  fails because of  4.f4  @@StartBracket@@  4...Qxb8  is answered by  5.Qf6#  @@EndBracket@@. }
2. Qg7#
{ the g8-rook is pinned.The chess term for describing a piece that has more defensive tasks than it can cope with is 'overloaded'. }
*

